&gt;&gt; I think that Tolstoy is still accurate
because there's a desire for

00:17:18.199 --> 00:17:22.602
self-heroism, [SOUND] and that's what they
focus upon.

00:17:22.602 --> 00:17:26.210
[SOUND] They don't focus upon the mud and

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:31.905
the blood and the mistakes and the things
that they did wrong.

00:17:31.905 --> 00:17:34.490
You very rarely hear about them.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:38.010
They, they focus on those moments when
there was a clear challenge and

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:39.130
they overcame it.

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:40.645
So we do have a tendency as-

00:17:40.645 --> 00:17:43.480
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; Human beings to, to compartment what

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:47.295
we want to remember, or at least what we
wanna share with anybody else.

00:17:47.295 --> 00:17:55.877
[SOUND].

00:17:55.877 --> 00:17:57.665
&gt;&gt; In War and Peace, there are no heroes.

00:17:57.665 --> 00:17:58.173
&gt;&gt; What shall we fire, Captain?

00:17:58.173 --> 00:17:59.065
&gt;&gt; Nothing.
A shell!

00:17:59.065 --> 00:18:01.790
&gt;&gt; The characters struggle with the
absurdity of life.

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:07.844
[SOUND] There is no reliable link between
actions and consequences.

00:18:07.844 --> 00:18:11.215
The virtuous are not rewarded.

00:18:11.215 --> 00:18:15.890
&gt;&gt; Captain Tushin, you've been ordered to
fall back.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:20.751
&gt;&gt; Tushin's battery is effective in
battle, but

00:18:20.751 --> 00:18:24.645
he is denounced to Prince Bagration.

00:18:24.645 --> 00:18:28.851
&gt;&gt; How was it that the artillery was
abandoned?

00:18:28.851 --> 00:18:31.553
&gt;&gt; I don't know, Your Excellency.

00:18:31.553 --> 00:18:34.350
&gt;&gt; In Iraq, no one has failed.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:37.020
I mean, every general has done brilliantly
and

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:39.955
been promoted and yet we've changed
strategy four times.

00:18:39.955 --> 00:18:41.060
[LAUGH].

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:44.430
Now this is one reason why you don't find
much learning there.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:48.880
Because if those who've come before, who
haven't done well, are promoted and

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:53.370
they're now your senior, you're not going
to criticize what they did.

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.645
You just change the strategy.

00:18:54.645 --> 00:18:55.250
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:57.100
&gt;&gt; Well this, this doesn't make a lot of
sense.

00:18:57.100 --> 00:19:02.617
And then the President gives medals of
freedom to people who've screwed up.

00:19:02.617 --> 00:19:05.130
&gt;&gt; I had no men, Your Excellency.

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:11.681
&gt;&gt; Tushin is rescued by Andrei Bolkonsky,
who demands that justice be done.

00:19:11.681 --> 00:19:14.729
&gt;&gt; Your Excellency!

00:19:14.729 --> 00:19:18.690
You sent me to Captain Tushin's battery
today.

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:20.497
I found two thirds of his me and

00:19:20.497 --> 00:19:25.496
his horses knocked out, two of his guns
disabled, and no covering troops at all.

00:19:25.496 --> 00:19:28.481
And if Your Excellency will permit me to
give my opinion,

00:19:28.481 --> 00:19:31.776
we owe today's success mainly to the
action of that battery and

00:19:31.776 --> 00:19:34.782
the heroic endurance of Captain Tushin and
his company.

00:19:34.782 --> 00:19:40.430
[SOUND]

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:46.952
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:19:46.952 --> 00:19:54.631
&gt;&gt; Captain Tushin, you may go.

00:19:54.631 --> 00:19:57.210
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, my friend.

00:19:57.210 --> 00:19:58.999
You saved me.

00:19:58.999 --> 00:20:03.980
&gt;&gt; Perhaps Captain Tushin deserves a
medal, but he will never get one.

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.320
&gt;&gt; Tushin had confidence.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.770
He knew how to do what he did and he did
it well.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:18.560
&gt;&gt; He's on the side of his friends who are
rationals.

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:19.242
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.

00:20:19.242 --> 00:20:21.030
&gt;&gt; Not, not much more.

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:21.900
&gt;&gt; And that's all.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:23.830
&gt;&gt; He's going to do a good job.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.970
&gt;&gt; When he deals with the higher
authorities, they ignore him.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:28.986
&gt;&gt; He's not solving problems.

00:20:28.986 --> 00:20:30.940
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; It's they who are solving it.

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:33.050
&gt;&gt; From history's point of view they're
nothing.

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:35.130
&gt;&gt; Tushins, many Tushins.

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:35.650
&gt;&gt; Tushins.

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.560
&gt;&gt; Not one Tushin, but Tushin apart.

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:38.630
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes, yes.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:43.870
&gt;&gt; In War and Peace, history is not made
by individual heroes,

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:46.460
little people, or leaders.

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:48.636
On the eve of the Borodino battle,

00:20:48.636 --> 00:20:53.824
Andrei ridicules Pierre's idea that a
skilled commander can make a difference.

00:20:53.824 --> 00:20:55.328
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:20:55.328 --> 00:20:57.572
&gt;&gt; He says, what faces us tomorrow?

00:20:57.572 --> 00:21:01.100
A hundred million of the most varied
possibilities,

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:05.920
which'll be decided instantly, by who
runs, or will run, away.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.630
Theirs or ours.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:10.220
By whether this one, or that, is killed.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:17.189
&gt;&gt; Look, that one almost blasted our
gentleman's hat off.

00:21:17.189 --> 00:21:18.612
&gt;&gt; The basic idea.

00:21:18.612 --> 00:21:24.952
History involves a complex system of
interacting actors and actions [SOUND].

00:21:24.952 --> 00:21:28.280
War and Peace tells us unnoticed causes
are important.

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:33.390
Some phenomena might be predictable, but
not controllable.

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:37.100
Why did Moscow burn?

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:42.380
Not because specific people ignited it,
but because people abandoned it.

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:44.420
Once abandoned, it would burn.

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:48.652
&gt;&gt; So, the idea of objective in my own
writing of history.

00:21:48.652 --> 00:21:51.914
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; I realized at a certain point that I

00:21:51.914 --> 00:21:54.680
had come to an impossible point.

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.687
I could write no more because I could not
document and

00:21:57.687 --> 00:22:02.510
confirm everything, which was the standard
you're being forced against.

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:06.750
At some point historians have to become
fiction writers.

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:10.340
And good history has an element of fiction
in it, it has too.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:13.970
The issue is how much you struggle for

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:18.636
objectivity and how you can weave a series
of, of views together.

00:22:18.636 --> 00:22:24.780
&gt;&gt; Leave that woman alone!

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:27.188
&gt;&gt; History is absurd.

00:22:27.188 --> 00:22:29.680
After Pierre Bezukhov is capture in
Moscow,

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:34.300
the french officer interrogating him is
called to eat.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:36.809
So he casually sends his Pierre to be
shot.

00:22:36.809 --> 00:22:40.536
[MUSIC]

00:22:40.536 --> 00:22:47.998
The military judicial commission sentences

00:22:47.998 --> 00:22:54.678
the accused to death by shooting [SOUND].

00:22:54.678 --> 00:22:59.410
&gt;&gt; Leave the fat one!

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:03.980
&gt;&gt; But ultimately Pierre, for no
observable, reason is spared.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:12.550
&gt;&gt; Don't!

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:18.040
&gt;&gt; War and Peace tells us that history
cannot be controlled by individuals.

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:21.190
Innocence is better than cleverness.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:24.380
Passivity is better than ambition.

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:30.727
Figueres who appear in history cannot be
important [SOUND].

00:23:30.727 --> 00:23:33.348
&gt;&gt; How can you not be afraid, sir?

00:23:33.348 --> 00:23:34.596
&gt;&gt; And are you afraid?

00:23:34.596 --> 00:23:35.450
&gt;&gt; How else could it be?

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:37.216
&gt;&gt; It won't forgive you.

00:23:37.216 --> 00:23:39.127
It'll blow your guts out.

00:23:39.127 --> 00:23:41.788
[LAUGH].

00:23:41.788 --> 00:23:45.882
&gt;&gt; These generals displayed here in front
of the battle of

00:23:45.882 --> 00:23:50.160
Borodino museum are major Russian heroes
of the battle.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:56.790
These cannon, on the other hand, are
reminders of

00:23:56.790 --> 00:24:02.130
the overlooked importance of minor people
doing their jobs well.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:08.200
In War and Peace, ordinary competence in
mundane manners

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:12.498
is pictured as more significant than
genius in command.

00:24:12.498 --> 00:24:18.239
The quiet competence of the artillery
officer Tushin,

00:24:18.239 --> 00:24:22.850
this contrasted with the bombast of
irrelevant generals.

00:24:27.340 --> 00:24:32.170
In a modern world, ordinary competence is
found in the bureaucrats, the rules,

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:34.184
the complex networks of organizations.

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:39.222
We're at Copenhagen Business School, and

00:24:39.222 --> 00:24:45.010
I'm visiting Marianne Risberg, who's an
Academic Administrative something.

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:45.748
&gt;&gt; Yeah, officer.

00:24:45.748 --> 00:24:47.346
&gt;&gt; Officer, yes.

00:24:47.346 --> 00:24:52.200
And I visited this school for something
like 35 years, and

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:58.102
when I asked people who is indispensable,
they don't mention the dean,

00:24:58.102 --> 00:25:02.024
or the rector, they mention Marianne
Risberg.

00:25:02.024 --> 00:25:04.625
&gt;&gt; What do you do, Marianne?

00:25:04.625 --> 00:25:05.986
&gt;&gt; What do I do?

00:25:05.986 --> 00:25:06.941
It's not just one thing.

00:25:06.941 --> 00:25:08.098
It's many, many things.

00:25:08.098 --> 00:25:11.995
I know whom to call when it, for a
specific piece of information.

00:25:11.995 --> 00:25:12.558
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
So

00:25:12.558 --> 00:25:16.554
you have a network of contacts around the
school.

00:25:16.554 --> 00:25:17.163
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:25:17.163 --> 00:25:21.570
&gt;&gt; You've been here 20 years, ever since
you were three years old, as I understand.

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:25.790
&gt;&gt; Yes, yes.
&gt;&gt; So you have a memory of what has

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:27.785
happened and how it happened and how to do
things.

00:25:27.785 --> 00:25:28.690
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:25:28.690 --> 00:25:30.840
&gt;&gt; And if they had a problem they called
up Marianne and

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:33.490
said, this is a problem, do something
about it.

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:34.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And.

00:25:34.450 --> 00:25:38.580
&gt;&gt; We have leaders and we have what you
would refer to only as the little people.

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:39.170
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:41.650
&gt;&gt; But I see it as a kind of division of
knowledge.

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:44.180
Because,.

00:25:44.180 --> 00:25:45.698
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You have all of it and they
have-

00:25:45.698 --> 00:25:49.470
&gt;&gt; No, not necessarily, but it's division,
I know, I, I have knowledge of,

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:53.320
of a certain area, and they have of a
different area.

00:25:53.320 --> 00:25:55.804
And none of us can actually do without one
another.

00:25:55.804 --> 00:25:57.750
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:25:57.750 --> 00:26:01.533
By connecting disparate parts of the
institution's strengths,

00:26:01.533 --> 00:26:05.324
Marianne's networks help to build the
school's capabilities.

00:26:05.324 --> 00:26:08.290
[MUSIC]

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:11.330
Another person building capabilities is
Susan Erb.

00:26:15.010 --> 00:26:19.450
Susan has been teaching first grade at
Park Day School for 33 years.

00:26:21.026 --> 00:26:23.731
&gt;&gt; What's so bad about CO2, says
Templeton.

00:26:23.731 --> 00:26:25.090
&gt;&gt; it's-

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:26.500
&gt;&gt; What's so bad about CO2?

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:31.160
&gt;&gt; If there's too much we would die
because if we kept breathing it,

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:33.130
it's really bad for us.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:37.050
So the leaves suck it in so, and they let
out it, oxygen so-

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:38.020
&gt;&gt; You're both friends-

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:41.750
&gt;&gt; In the world of War and Peace, leaders
are unimportant.

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:42.930
But culture, and

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:47.380
shared values, are essential elements of a
nation's capabilities.

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:48.460
&gt;&gt; That's a strategy.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.050
What were you gonna do?

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:54.270
&gt;&gt; In the modern world, many of those
capabilities are produced by millions of

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:57.140
teachers, like Susan Erb, working in
schools.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:01.515
&gt;&gt; I need family, friends, food, and
water, peace in the world, and oxygen.

00:27:01.515 --> 00:27:05.671
And I want [INAUDIBLE].

00:27:05.671 --> 00:27:10.410
&gt;&gt; Susan molds the basic building blocks
of culture in her first graders.

00:27:10.410 --> 00:27:15.809
Self knowledge, understanding the physical
world, and

00:27:15.809 --> 00:27:20.886
the ability to get along with others in
the community.

00:27:20.886 --> 00:27:24.898
&gt;&gt; It's really up to you two.

00:27:24.898 --> 00:27:26.259
&gt;&gt; Break the record!

00:27:26.259 --> 00:27:28.820
&gt;&gt; Have we broken the rules in awhile?

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:30.485
&gt;&gt; No.

00:27:30.485 --> 00:27:32.455
&gt;&gt; Ok, ready, here we go.

00:27:32.455 --> 00:27:33.287
We're not there.

00:27:33.287 --> 00:27:39.993
[SOUND] Shh, they're doing something
important.

00:27:39.993 --> 00:27:42.452
They're breaking the rules.

00:27:42.452 --> 00:27:44.045
&gt;&gt; In the world view of War and

00:27:44.045 --> 00:27:48.827
Peace, Susan Erb, like Marianne Risberg in
Copenhagen and Captain Tushin,

00:27:48.827 --> 00:27:54.350
are part of the throng of millions of
competent people who really make history.

00:27:54.350 --> 00:27:57.466
These people who make a difference are
connected in

00:27:57.466 --> 00:28:02.117
complex social networks that give
structure to an apparently chaotic world

00:28:02.117 --> 00:28:06.547
[MUSIC]

00:28:06.547 --> 00:28:14.408
The Russian society of War and Peace is
based on elaborate networks of family and

00:28:14.408 --> 00:28:22.385
personal connections, thus the emphasis in
the novel on arranging marriages,

00:28:22.385 --> 00:28:28.633
on exploiting connections and, of course,
on this [UNKNOWN].

00:28:28.633 --> 00:28:31.550
&gt;&gt; I hear you have Pierre Bezukhov staying
with you.

00:28:31.550 --> 00:28:33.510
&gt;&gt; Well since he inherited Bezukhov's
estate,

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:36.320
I thought it my duty to take him under my
wing.

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.440
&gt;&gt; I do hope his manners have improved.

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:40.075
&gt;&gt; So rich nobody notices anymore.

00:28:40.075 --> 00:28:41.017
[LAUGH].

00:28:41.017 --> 00:28:44.085
&gt;&gt; There's Lisa Bolkonskaya now.

00:28:44.085 --> 00:28:46.290
&gt;&gt; Hm, adorable!

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:51.220
&gt;&gt; It is not hard to imagine in these
beautiful rooms,

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:56.110
in the Yusupov Palace in St. Petersburg, a
social gathering at which

00:28:56.110 --> 00:29:00.910
gossip would be exchanged, and favors
would be sought and granted.

00:29:00.910 --> 00:29:04.010
It was in places such as this that the
intricate

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:09.330
networks of aristocratic Russian life were
elaborated and maintained.

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:15.290
The primary instruments for maintaining
one's position in

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:19.960
the Russian social order of War and Peace
are marriage and inheritance.

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:22.184
&gt;&gt; He won't survive a third stroke.

00:29:22.184 --> 00:29:24.114
[MUSIC]

00:29:24.114 --> 00:29:28.150
You know, Katishe that you and my wife are
his only direct heirs.

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:30.530
It's painful I know, still one must talk.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:33.830
&gt;&gt; I never ceased praying to God that he'd
be merciful.

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:35.930
&gt;&gt; Still we must be ready.

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.940
I sent for Pierre, you know.

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:39.640
Well, he asked for him.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:41.310
&gt;&gt; What does he want him for?

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:43.150
He's illegitimate, he can't inherit
anything.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:47.150
&gt;&gt; I've tried to explain to you before,
all the old count had to do was to

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:50.016
write a letter to the Czar, begging him to
make Pierre legitimate.

00:29:50.016 --> 00:29:55.199
&gt;&gt; With Prince Vasili's help, Pierre
receives his inheritance.

00:29:55.199 --> 00:29:59.919
Vasili then manipulates the newly wealthy
young man into marrying his

00:29:59.919 --> 00:30:01.202
daughter Helene.

00:30:01.202 --> 00:30:02.525
It's going to happen.

00:30:02.525 --> 00:30:04.545
I know it.

00:30:04.545 --> 00:30:05.790
They're all expecting it.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.660
They're so certain it will happen.

00:30:09.660 --> 00:30:12.124
I can't disappoint them.

00:30:12.124 --> 00:30:13.760
&gt;&gt; Well.

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.626
&gt;&gt; Nothing.

00:30:16.626 --> 00:30:20.429
&gt;&gt; [NOISE]

00:30:20.429 --> 00:30:28.798
My dear boy.

00:30:28.798 --> 00:30:30.369
Thank god.

00:30:30.369 --> 00:30:32.310
My wife has just told me.

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:34.080
Oh my, I'm so happy.

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:36.860
I knew your father.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:37.830
I loved him I must say.

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:42.730
Oh, you make her such a good wife.

00:30:42.730 --> 00:30:44.290
My little girl.

00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:46.270
My little Elaine.

00:30:46.270 --> 00:30:47.890
Eileen, Eileen, Anna come here.

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:53.190
Oh this is wonderful, wonderful.

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:56.240
&gt;&gt; War and Peace portrays a world
permeated with falsity.

00:30:57.420 --> 00:30:59.160
It is not however a world of chaos.

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:05.837
Aristocratic, family networks and a stable
peasant culture infused Russian life.

00:31:05.837 --> 00:31:11.302
[MUSIC]

00:31:11.302 --> 00:31:16.734
These elements bring to the unfolding of
history an overall capability,

00:31:16.734 --> 00:31:20.423
the General Kutuzov often called Mother
Russia.

00:31:20.423 --> 00:31:23.878
[MUSIC]

00:31:23.878 --> 00:31:26.379
War and Peace pictures peasant life and

00:31:26.379 --> 00:31:29.720
traditions as the core of Russian
strength.

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:34.010
&gt;&gt; Oh but you should see what a fine job
they made of the old place now.

00:31:34.010 --> 00:31:36.940
A yard full of livestock.

00:31:36.940 --> 00:31:39.260
A field full of oats.

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:41.395
Two other brothers earning wages.

00:31:41.395 --> 00:31:46.205
[LAUGH] It's a treat to go home to.

00:31:46.205 --> 00:31:48.160
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].
&gt;&gt; The peasant brings a culture of

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:49.505
elementary values and

00:31:49.505 --> 00:31:54.046
knowledge that transcends the cleverness
and pretensions of leadership.

00:31:54.046 --> 00:31:58.108
[MUSIC]

00:31:58.108 --> 00:32:03.323
&gt;&gt; Behind me sits [INAUDIBLE] a Russian
village of old believers located about

00:32:03.323 --> 00:32:06.941
a 1,000 kilometers northeast of Moscow,
and

00:32:06.941 --> 00:32:10.740
about 500 kilometers west of the Ural
Mountains.

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:17.000
It consists of about 45 occupied houses,
with about 100 people.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:22.320
We've come here in search of Mother
Russia,

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:25.960
that mysterious combination of habits,
patience, and

00:32:25.960 --> 00:32:32.800
confidence that describes the culture of
traditional peasant life.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:38.940
In War and Peace the Russian General
Kutuzov, views Mother Russia

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:43.988
both as a teacher and as an ally in his
struggles against the French.

00:32:43.988 --> 00:32:47.679
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]

00:32:47.679 --> 00:32:54.550
&gt;&gt; You have to be able to curse a blue
streak here, and be able to drink.

00:32:54.550 --> 00:33:00.405
Yeah, they taught me how to swear of
course.

00:33:00.405 --> 00:33:01.530
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:05.970
&gt;&gt; The essence of peasant living in
endurance through the turnover of regimes.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:12.520
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:17.659
&gt;&gt; Peasant life is buffeted by change but
it allows the waves of monarchs and

00:33:17.659 --> 00:33:22.390
masters to pass over it while holding on
to the ability to survive.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:27.042
[MUSIC]

00:33:27.042 --> 00:33:28.220
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:30.008
&gt;&gt; I can't be without the Earth.

00:33:30.008 --> 00:33:31.210
It's the thing that feeds us.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:33.170
It's our birth mother.

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:35.160
How my heart pours out.

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:37.010
Everything now is fallow.

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:37.710
I cry.

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:39.070
I can't sleep at night.

00:33:39.070 --> 00:33:44.310
Look my hair

00:33:44.310 --> 00:33:51.306
is turning gray, it's just awful.

00:33:51.306 --> 00:33:54.708
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:33:54.708 --> 00:33:56.852
&gt;&gt; He used a huge, he used an old Samovar
with you know,

00:33:56.852 --> 00:33:57.492
with the [UNKNOWN]

00:33:57.492 --> 00:33:59.380
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; Because his own honey.

00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:01.890
&gt;&gt; Done.
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:01.890 --> 00:34:02.820
&gt;&gt; War and Peace

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:07.530
offers numerous minor critiques of peasant
life.

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:08.200
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; But

00:34:08.200 --> 00:34:12.280
one of the books main themes is a
throughly romantic portrayal

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:16.895
of the peasant, his abilities, and his
connection to the land.

00:34:16.895 --> 00:34:23.978
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; We never lived in the city.

00:34:23.978 --> 00:34:25.680
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]

00:34:25.680 --> 00:34:29.490
&gt;&gt; You know for me it's always been the
power of nature.

00:34:29.490 --> 00:34:33.010
Where I was born, our cabin was right next
to the woods.

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:34.070
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:40.450
&gt;&gt; He was born just like Jesus Christ, in
a manger, in the hay with the sheep.

00:34:40.450 --> 00:34:42.160
&gt;&gt; My mother gave birth.

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:43.200
&gt;&gt; It was a big family.

00:34:45.410 --> 00:34:47.530
&gt;&gt; My father really loved nature and

00:34:47.530 --> 00:34:53.440
he passed it along to us as an inheritance
through our genes, and I love nature too.

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:58.170
When I was just a kid, he gave me a little
ax, an ax, and

00:34:58.170 --> 00:35:03.660
as we were walking, I chipped at the trees
in the woods with it.

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:09.468
He was very strict and said don't ever do
that, ever.

00:35:09.468 --> 00:35:11.880
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; I'm ashamed before nature if I

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:12.910
take heedlessly.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:16.620
I gather all the twigs I knock down for
firewood.

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:17.908
And then I feel good.

00:35:17.908 --> 00:35:27.908
[MUSIC]

00:35:30.993 --> 00:35:32.964
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:35:32.964 --> 00:35:35.139
&gt;&gt; There was war, there was famine.

00:35:35.139 --> 00:35:37.595
This icon here is from my mother.

00:35:37.595 --> 00:35:41.585
The icon of Jesus, she set it up for me
and my sister and

00:35:41.585 --> 00:35:45.110
said, say your prayers to Jesus girls.

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:45.673
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]
&gt;&gt; And she would, and

00:35:45.673 --> 00:35:46.377
she would tell us, to me and my sister.

00:35:46.377 --> 00:35:52.970
&gt;&gt; Our father died, he died of hunger.

00:35:52.970 --> 00:36:01.040
We're crying and praying how could it not
help us.

00:36:01.040 --> 00:36:02.065
&gt;&gt; What did people do.

00:36:02.065 --> 00:36:03.080
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:06.538
&gt;&gt; We ate other things leaves, we
collected grasses.

00:36:06.538 --> 00:36:09.690
&gt;&gt; There was famine.

00:36:09.690 --> 00:36:14.968
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:36:14.968 --> 00:36:19.640
&gt;&gt; People have their own households.

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:22.110
Whoever keeps a cow lives well.

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:23.020
That's how we survived.

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.870
Whoever loves the land lives off it.

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:33.258
And if you don't love the land and till
it, then it's all over.

00:36:33.258 --> 00:36:34.424
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:36:34.424 --> 00:36:44.424
[MUSIC]

00:36:55.472 --> 00:36:59.753
&gt;&gt; It it not necessary to accept the
romantic peasant mystique,

00:36:59.753 --> 00:37:05.107
to recognize the more general point that
leadership involves developing and

00:37:05.107 --> 00:37:08.008
using the values and spirit of a culture.

00:37:08.008 --> 00:37:18.008
[MUSIC]

00:37:43.859 --> 00:37:48.134
In defeating the invading French, General
Kutuzov depended on

00:37:48.134 --> 00:37:53.260
the spirit of Russian peasants, and their
capacity to survive.

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:56.715
That spirit finds expression in dance.

00:37:56.715 --> 00:38:03.413
[NOISE] This is the rehearsal hall of the
song and

00:38:03.413 --> 00:38:09.425
dance ensemble of the Russian army in St.

00:38:09.425 --> 00:38:14.750
Petersburg, a group of soldiers and

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:22.508
civilians who perform traditional Russian
dances.

00:38:22.508 --> 00:38:28.705
[NOISE].

00:38:28.705 --> 00:38:30.838
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:38:30.838 --> 00:38:32.870
&gt;&gt; It's really from very long ago.

00:38:32.870 --> 00:38:36.610
The whole history of Russia is connected
to these dances.

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:40.700
Our grandfathers all of them and our great
grandfathers they all danced.

00:38:40.700 --> 00:38:44.876
The Russian spirit supports it, it
improves your self esteem.

00:38:44.876 --> 00:38:51.642
[MUSIC]

00:38:51.642 --> 00:38:53.817
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:38:53.817 --> 00:38:57.581
&gt;&gt; Unfortunately now it's become rarer,
even in the villages,

00:38:57.581 --> 00:39:02.350
to see real people dance Russian folk
dance who haven't studied it specially.

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:05.539
So we are trying, somehow, to preserve
what our ancestors did.

00:39:05.539 --> 00:39:12.813
[MUSIC]

00:39:12.813 --> 00:39:17.222
&gt;&gt; My father's a dance master.

00:39:17.222 --> 00:39:20.797
My brother and grandfather were both
artists.

00:39:20.797 --> 00:39:23.357
It's like I've gone down the family path.

00:39:23.357 --> 00:39:33.357
[MUSIC]

00:39:47.126 --> 00:39:48.144
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:39:48.144 --> 00:39:50.886
&gt;&gt; It's the soul, that's where it comes
from.

00:39:50.886 --> 00:39:51.820
Without this, a Russian is not a Russian.

00:39:56.760 --> 00:39:58.970
&gt;&gt; Theres another theme in the War on
Peace,

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:03.720
of the importance of the competency of
ordinary people.

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:08.520
That history is, is created by [COUGH]
complex combinations of

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:12.890
a lot of ordinary people doing their jobs.

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:17.250
&gt;&gt; We talked with sociologist Joel
Padolme, Dean of the Yale School of

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:22.850
Management, to get his perspective on
leadership, capabilities and networks.

00:40:22.850 --> 00:40:28.960
&gt;&gt; It's one of the unfortunate facts of
the modern literature on leadership that

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:36.420
we do tend to over-play the importance of
a single individual, the heroic leader.

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:42.350
History gives a leader certain artifacts
with which to work.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:47.410
And one of those artifacts is clearly the
pattern of relationships,

00:40:47.410 --> 00:40:50.210
the networks that exists.

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:54.550
A savvy leader is going to know that she
or

00:40:54.550 --> 00:41:00.397
he is going to be a lot more effective if
she tries to understand

00:41:00.397 --> 00:41:05.910
what's the, what's the underlying fabric
of relationships.

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:08.862
And then sort of work with that fabric.

00:41:08.862 --> 00:41:11.126
[SOUND].

00:41:11.126 --> 00:41:14.901
&gt;&gt; War and Peace argues that heroic
leadership has little to do

00:41:14.901 --> 00:41:16.950
with the unfolding of history.

00:41:19.350 --> 00:41:23.670
However the novel hints at a different
role for leaders.

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:27.120
Russian aristocratic society at the
beginning of the 19th

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:33.940
century was a network of extended families
connected by reciprocal favors.

00:41:33.940 --> 00:41:40.010
In such a society effective leadership
involves exploiting social networks for

00:41:40.010 --> 00:41:41.720
the capabilities they offer.

00:41:44.420 --> 00:41:47.350
&gt;&gt; That perspective has overtones in

00:41:47.350 --> 00:41:51.410
the 15th century history of Renaissance
Florence.

00:41:51.410 --> 00:41:56.555
And we've come to Florence now to talk
with Professor John Pageant,

00:41:56.555 --> 00:42:02.598
a leading scholar of the 15th century
Florentine world of Cosimo de' Medici.

00:42:02.598 --> 00:42:05.807
[MUSIC]

00:42:05.807 --> 00:42:10.250
&gt;&gt; Cosimo de' Medici did not begin with a
grand plan to take over the state.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:12.690
Rather events sort of swept him into
power.

00:42:12.690 --> 00:42:17.480
It was his networks, his banking networks
that made Venice so happy to see him come,

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:20.590
family networks that pulled the people in
the street, all these sort of

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:24.020
organizational networks [UNKNOWN]
capabilities, you might call them.

00:42:24.020 --> 00:42:26.110
It didn't allow him to initiate action,
but

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:29.200
definitely allowed him to respond to
action.

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:33.150
Now there a number of perspectives from
which you can view Cosimo de' Medici.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:37.560
The basis of his economic power, was the
fact that he was a banker to the Pope.

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:42.960
But he translated this Papal connection
into organizational capacity, and

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:49.830
he funded all sorts of international trade
throughout Europe.

00:42:49.830 --> 00:42:53.640
This room is very similar to what the
account book room

00:42:53.640 --> 00:42:55.680
of the Medici bank would have looked like.

00:42:55.680 --> 00:42:58.694
The Florentine bankers and their social
networks are,

00:42:58.694 --> 00:43:03.384
it transfers through which customers all
over Europe are conducting their business.

00:43:03.384 --> 00:43:11.752
[MUSIC]

00:43:11.752 --> 00:43:15.929
Is it of great deal of trust and security
involved that has to be established for

00:43:15.929 --> 00:43:17.330
this system to work.

00:43:17.330 --> 00:43:23.148
Account books exist as a, as a sign, as a
symbol, so each of these pages is actually

00:43:23.148 --> 00:43:28.475
a social network, it's a person with whom
the, the banker is doing business.

00:43:28.475 --> 00:43:34.366
[MUSIC]

00:43:34.366 --> 00:43:39.836
&gt;&gt; One of the most familiar scenes in War
and Peace is people waiting outside

00:43:39.836 --> 00:43:45.395
an influential person's door, to seek
favors or to bring information or

00:43:45.395 --> 00:43:51.960
to tell them that they are their friends,
that's the way the social structure works.

00:43:53.010 --> 00:43:57.930
We're in Florence outside of Cosimo de
Medici's house.

00:43:57.930 --> 00:44:04.160
I'm sitting on the bench where similar
people would have sat in the 15th century.

00:44:04.160 --> 00:44:06.150
Waiting to bring things to Cosimo.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:08.740
Waiting to ask him for favors.

00:44:08.740 --> 00:44:12.828
Waiting to enter into the basic game of

00:44:12.828 --> 00:44:17.950
the social network that Cosimo presided
over.

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:25.368
[MUSIC]

00:44:25.368 --> 00:44:30.600
&gt;&gt; Jim we are here in the private chapel
of the Palazzo de' Medici.

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:34.960
In other words, in the inner sanctum of
Cosimo's own home.

00:44:34.960 --> 00:44:36.870
It's the Journey of the Magi.

00:44:36.870 --> 00:44:41.370
The image of the Magi became developed,
you might say, as the mythology that de'

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:45.520
Medici used to present themselves to their
fellow citizens of Florence.

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:49.370
Cosimo de' Medici, the gentleman on the
donkey, probably thinks of

00:44:49.370 --> 00:44:55.360
himself also as a humble man, in spite of
his incredible wealth and power.

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:56.590
So-
&gt;&gt; Donkey with guilded

00:44:57.700 --> 00:44:59.330
trekkings of course.

00:44:59.330 --> 00:44:59.859
&gt;&gt; Good point.

00:44:59.859 --> 00:45:00.555
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:45:00.555 --> 00:45:04.829
&gt;&gt; And a precession of a journey of the
Medici chief family going back.

00:45:04.829 --> 00:45:08.716
Through time to this castle up there on
the hill.

00:45:08.716 --> 00:45:15.051
And going forward in time through
following the, the Magi to the baby Jesus.

00:45:15.051 --> 00:45:19.867
So to understand the process of
constructing family, and by extension

00:45:19.867 --> 00:45:25.660
political alliances, it is quite important
to think about this practice of giving.

00:45:25.660 --> 00:45:27.719
Gift exchange, food, money,

00:45:27.719 --> 00:45:32.602
building, this is the raw materials, the
raw organizational practices,

00:45:32.602 --> 00:45:37.120
if you will that the Florentines used to
build, their networks.

00:45:37.120 --> 00:45:44.549
[MUSIC]

00:45:44.549 --> 00:45:50.680
I think in the Medici story that Paget
tells so well.

00:45:50.680 --> 00:45:55.960
I mean, the real brilliance that's there
is him recognizing, that in point of fact,

00:45:55.960 --> 00:46:03.300
if you build a network that is goal
directed, it has two problems.

00:46:03.300 --> 00:46:06.420
One, it's very costly.

00:46:06.420 --> 00:46:08.957
Because in order to get people to sign up
for

00:46:08.957 --> 00:46:12.279
that they know exactly the terms that they
can extract.

00:46:12.279 --> 00:46:13.777
Two, if your goals or

00:46:13.777 --> 00:46:19.195
your interests ever shift, then you've
created this very narrowly defined

00:46:19.195 --> 00:46:24.968
network that's now focused on doing this
when you want to go in in that direction.

00:46:24.968 --> 00:46:34.968
[MUSIC]

00:46:47.804 --> 00:46:51.779
Seems to me there are two sort of basic
ways in which people try to

00:46:51.779 --> 00:46:53.430
make themselves endure.

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:55.750
The first is family.

00:46:57.280 --> 00:47:01.580
Through a family, your genetic and
cultural trace goes to your children, and

00:47:01.580 --> 00:47:03.790
from your children to their children, and
then so on and

00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:05.710
so on, through the generations.

00:47:05.710 --> 00:47:11.100
The second major way, is through the
artifacts we create.

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:13.960
We make various forms of art.

00:47:13.960 --> 00:47:17.620
Painting, sculpture, writings of various
sorts.

00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:18.830
And buildings.

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:21.540
The various things that we call, art.

00:47:22.864 --> 00:47:24.649
And these endure long [UNKNOWN].

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:31.278
Now Cosimo obviously was involved in both
of these.

00:47:31.278 --> 00:47:36.648
Is there any contemporaneous evidence that
he was concerned

00:47:36.648 --> 00:47:42.238
either about his relevance to history or
about his mortality?

00:47:42.238 --> 00:47:45.810
&gt;&gt; Actually Jim the answer to that is very
much yes.

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:47.420
According to Vespasiano,

00:47:47.420 --> 00:47:51.730
he thought that people would never
remember him five years after he died.

00:47:51.730 --> 00:47:56.215
The only way that they would remember him
is through this art and these buildings,

00:47:56.215 --> 00:47:59.678
in particularly these buildings that he
made to influence his children,

00:47:59.678 --> 00:48:02.218
the grandchildren, us.

00:48:02.218 --> 00:48:06.440
The building La Cupola, the dome in the
middle, was not started by Cosimo but

00:48:06.440 --> 00:48:07.950
was finished by Cosimo.

00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:13.860
San Lorenzo was actually renovated and
beautified by Cosimo.

00:48:13.860 --> 00:48:18.320
Badia the abbey and monasteries in the
hills were made by Cozomo.

00:48:18.320 --> 00:48:21.830
He made his palaces and started an entire
palace movement.

00:48:21.830 --> 00:48:27.067
So if you put all of these things
together, banker, statesman, patron,

00:48:27.067 --> 00:48:31.738
I think you have the conclusion and the
image of a Renaissance man.

00:48:31.738 --> 00:48:33.691
And Cosimo started a regime,

00:48:33.691 --> 00:48:39.430
the Medicean regime that lasted almost
four centuries with one small break.

00:48:39.430 --> 00:48:44.700
&gt;&gt; In some sense you have a kind of goal
free problem solving.

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:50.210
You are creating an institution or
creating some capabilities or creating

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:57.070
some answers without quite knowing what
the questions are going to be.

00:48:57.070 --> 00:49:01.700
&gt;&gt; There is this idea with vision that
it's always about looking forward.

00:49:01.700 --> 00:49:06.760
But I think if you look at the really
powerful visions that get developed to

00:49:06.760 --> 00:49:13.010
lead groups, to lead social movement,
they're typically visions

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:19.640
that are grounded in a very strong sense
of common identity, with strong historical

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:25.400
roots and what the, what the leader has
done is to interpret that for everybody.

00:49:25.400 --> 00:49:30.380
And that act of interpretation actually
becomes the basis for

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:33.860
building and reinforcing the network.

00:49:33.860 --> 00:49:37.250
&gt;&gt; In many ways that's General Kutuzov in
War and Peace.

00:49:37.250 --> 00:49:42.952
He talks about the soul of Mother Russia.

00:49:42.952 --> 00:49:52.952
[MUSIC]

00:50:10.490 --> 00:50:12.137
[FOREIGN].

00:50:12.137 --> 00:50:15.692
&gt;&gt; In War and Peace, advance planning and
strategies to

00:50:15.692 --> 00:50:20.996
change the course of history are pictured
as the pretenses of foolish leaders.

00:50:20.996 --> 00:50:24.258
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:50:24.258 --> 00:50:28.658
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:50:28.658 --> 00:50:32.090
&gt;&gt; General Kutuzov's wisdom on the other
hand,

00:50:32.090 --> 00:50:35.171
involves going with the flow of history.

00:50:35.171 --> 00:50:45.171
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:50:46.896 --> 00:50:55.430
&gt;&gt; [NOISE]
[FOREIGN].

00:50:55.430 --> 00:50:58.750
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:50:58.750 --> 00:50:59.691
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:50:59.691 --> 00:51:03.789
&gt;&gt; At Austerlitz he knows all the well
laid plans are for naught.

00:51:03.789 --> 00:51:10.893
After Bordeno, Kutuzoff retreats and waits
patiently for Mother Russia to prevail.

00:51:10.893 --> 00:51:12.471
[NOISE].

00:51:12.471 --> 00:51:17.406
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:51:17.406 --> 00:51:19.742
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN]

00:51:19.742 --> 00:51:27.420
Napoleon has left Moscow?

00:51:30.280 --> 00:51:32.350
Huh?

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:42.360
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:51:42.360 --> 00:51:47.180
&gt;&gt; In contemporary terms Kutuzov's wisdom
is mystical or instinctive.

00:51:47.180 --> 00:51:51.230
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:51:51.230 --> 00:51:54.070
&gt;&gt; Comprehension cannot be sought.

00:51:54.070 --> 00:51:58.020
It comes in great random moments that
cannot be anticipated.

00:51:59.300 --> 00:52:00.541
&gt;&gt; How peaceful.

00:52:00.541 --> 00:52:01.704
&gt;&gt; In bringing about a result-

00:52:01.704 --> 00:52:07.530
&gt;&gt; In that spirit, General Kutuzov
abandons claims of self-importance and

00:52:07.530 --> 00:52:10.770
accepts the ironies of life and history.

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:16.670
&gt;&gt; Why did you not pursue the French
across the frontier?

00:52:16.670 --> 00:52:21.000
&gt;&gt; My instructions were to expel the
invader from our soil.

00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:24.300
&gt;&gt; Can you really be said to have
accomplished your objectives by halting

00:52:24.300 --> 00:52:26.020
arbitrarily at the frontiers of our
country?

00:52:27.050 --> 00:52:29.240
You have expelled the enemy from Russia.

00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:34.652
Now it is for us to take over and continue
your great work.

00:52:34.652 --> 00:52:38.738
And from now on, I shall assume command of
the army, myself.

00:52:38.738 --> 00:52:48.738
[SOUND].

00:52:54.655 --> 00:53:04.655
[MUSIC]

00:53:35.694 --> 00:53:36.304
&gt;&gt; War and

00:53:36.304 --> 00:53:42.927
Peace scorns those who imagine acting
decisively in pursuit of a grand plan.

00:53:42.927 --> 00:53:47.768
It venerates those who are strong enough
to be patiently passive.

00:53:47.768 --> 00:53:50.504
And it praises those who can take
advantage of

00:53:50.504 --> 00:53:52.667
whatever strengths they may have.

00:53:52.667 --> 00:53:55.407
[MUSIC]

00:53:55.407 --> 00:54:05.407
[SOUND].

00:54:07.226 --> 00:54:09.188
[MUSIC]

00:54:09.188 --> 00:54:14.568
Like Kutuzov, Cosimo de' Medici positioned
himself to take advantage of the winds,

00:54:14.568 --> 00:54:17.160
rather than trying to sail against them.

00:54:18.170 --> 00:54:22.920
He built capabilities without anticipating
clearly what would be done with them.

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:30.635
Cosimo spent part of each day meditating
in his cell at the San Marco monastery.

00:54:30.635 --> 00:54:32.885
[SOUND].

00:54:32.885 --> 00:54:37.740
&gt;&gt; The entry point that all monks and
Cosimo himself would arrive at-

00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:41.270
&gt;&gt; This [UNKNOWN] angelical painting of
the enunciation,

00:54:41.270 --> 00:54:46.320
which Cosimo commissioned, greeted him
every day on the way to his cell.

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:48.970
&gt;&gt; In this particular awesome place.

00:54:48.970 --> 00:54:52.870
&gt;&gt; Mary is listening very hard to what the
angel is saying.

00:54:52.870 --> 00:54:55.658
And this is the attitude that monks ought
to put on

00:54:55.658 --> 00:54:58.520
when they enter this particular
establishment.

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:00.760
[MUSIC]

00:55:00.760 --> 00:55:03.128
Mary is listening.

00:55:03.128 --> 00:55:04.810
The angel is talking, or

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:09.306
trying to pick up what the angel is saying
by listening to himself.

00:55:09.306 --> 00:55:19.306
[MUSIC]

00:55:36.901 --> 00:55:41.975
&gt;&gt; Perhaps leaders do not change the
course of history but they construct

00:55:41.975 --> 00:55:47.829
the buildings and commission the art and
architecture that enhances our lives.

00:55:49.050 --> 00:55:55.030
Cosimo de' Medici was the patron of the
great Florentine sculptor Donatello.

00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:59.285
This room is filled with objects produced
by

00:55:59.285 --> 00:56:05.153
Donatello during his life as a client of
Cosimo de' Medici.

00:56:05.153 --> 00:56:15.153
[MUSIC]

00:56:37.997 --> 00:56:43.109
War and Peace tells us that beauty is
found not only in the high art of

00:56:43.109 --> 00:56:47.689
great artists, but also in the textures of
everyday life.

00:56:47.689 --> 00:56:54.355
[MUSIC]

00:56:54.355 --> 00:57:00.089
Beauty is found in the hunt, in sleigh
rides.

00:57:00.089 --> 00:57:02.215
In dance.

00:57:02.215 --> 00:57:08.656
[MUSIC]

00:57:08.656 --> 00:57:10.154
And in family relations.

00:57:10.154 --> 00:57:14.671
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

00:57:14.671 --> 00:57:19.507
&gt;&gt; In modern life, beauty is found in a
Florentine restaurant,

00:57:19.507 --> 00:57:25.350
where Giuseppe Alessi recreates recipes
from the 15th century.

00:57:25.350 --> 00:57:30.590
He imbues them not only with extraordinary
flavors, but also with his enthusiasm.

00:57:30.590 --> 00:57:34.620
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN] I use the power of fire.

00:57:34.620 --> 00:57:37.190
My relationship with fire is based on
conflict,

00:57:37.190 --> 00:57:40.105
because clearly fire devours everything.

00:57:40.105 --> 00:57:40.820
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:40.820 --> 00:57:43.045
&gt;&gt; I have to dominate the fire, and

00:57:43.045 --> 00:57:47.830
beat it back with iron, to make the food
just right.

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:54.370
&gt;&gt; Beauty is found within the harsh
wartime life of a Russian village.

00:57:54.370 --> 00:58:00.270
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN] And the war was still going
on, but it makes me want to cry.

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.334
We had nothing to wear.

00:58:02.334 --> 00:58:04.413
There was nothing to eat.

00:58:04.413 --> 00:58:08.058
Then they sent us packages to the town
here.

00:58:08.058 --> 00:58:11.993
Even today I can remember the taste of
that sugar.

00:58:11.993 --> 00:58:18.575
[MUSIC]

00:58:18.575 --> 00:58:24.430
So when America sent us some rags, we were
very happy.

00:58:24.430 --> 00:58:28.480
Even today I remember there was a checked
skirt with a very fine check.

00:58:28.480 --> 00:58:33.898
And whenever I see that kind of material
immediately I remember.

00:58:33.898 --> 00:58:35.760
[FOREIGN].

00:58:35.760 --> 00:58:37.467
&gt;&gt; In fact I remember almost all of-

00:58:37.467 --> 00:58:38.664
This was my brother's shirt.

00:58:38.664 --> 00:58:40.323
I can remember that.

00:58:40.323 --> 00:58:43.446
I can remember that this was a skirt that
my mom wore it.

00:58:43.446 --> 00:58:46.882
But a lot of these pieces on here I
remember.

00:58:46.882 --> 00:58:50.740
&gt;&gt; Beauty is found in the attitude toward
life of Oran Ohl,

00:58:50.740 --> 00:58:55.667
who makes quilts to restore good feelings
after the deaths of victims of

00:58:55.667 --> 00:58:59.048
urban violence, including two of her own
sons.

00:58:59.048 --> 00:59:09.063
[MUSIC]

00:59:09.063 --> 00:59:12.140
Well I'm so glad you guys came.

00:59:12.140 --> 00:59:15.007
And now you know how to redirect that
inner anger.

00:59:15.007 --> 00:59:16.644
Once you turn it in, let it out.

00:59:16.644 --> 00:59:17.283
See what I did?

00:59:17.283 --> 00:59:19.419
I let it all out.

00:59:19.419 --> 00:59:22.200
Now its all out there for the public to
see.

00:59:22.200 --> 00:59:24.730
It didn't hurt anybody, right?

00:59:24.730 --> 00:59:26.330
If anything, it helps.

00:59:26.330 --> 00:59:30.722
So you can always find something to do to
redirect that inner pain and

00:59:30.722 --> 00:59:32.659
make it into something good.

00:59:32.659 --> 00:59:34.145
Don't let it stay in there.

00:59:34.145 --> 00:59:36.386
Find something to get it out.

00:59:36.386 --> 00:59:37.779
You know, in a positive way.

00:59:37.779 --> 00:59:47.930
[MUSIC]

00:59:47.930 --> 00:59:54.295
War and Peace tells us that the passion,
the serenity, and the elementary

00:59:54.295 --> 01:00:00.571
grace of millions of ordinary people
create the beauty of everyday life.

01:00:00.571 --> 01:00:05.263
This room, in the Yusupov palace in St.
Petersburg.

01:00:05.263 --> 01:00:07.384
Is a reminder that War and

01:00:07.384 --> 01:00:13.950
Peace embraces an aesthetic response to
the images and experiences of life.

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:17.840
No where is the dedication of War and

01:00:17.840 --> 01:00:23.540
Peace to beauty more evident than in the
character of Natasha Rostova.

01:00:23.540 --> 01:00:26.630
&gt;&gt; Tolstoy is obviously in love with
Natasha Rastova.

01:00:27.710 --> 01:00:28.370
&gt;&gt; It's true.

01:00:28.370 --> 01:00:29.550
&gt;&gt; She's frivolous.

01:00:29.550 --> 01:00:31.890
She's narcissistic.

01:00:31.890 --> 01:00:34.301
She's self-indulgent.

01:00:34.301 --> 01:00:36.140
And yet, he's in love with her.

01:00:36.140 --> 01:00:37.220
&gt;&gt; But she's natural.

01:00:37.220 --> 01:00:38.114
&gt;&gt; She is natural.

01:00:38.114 --> 01:00:38.650
You know?

01:00:38.650 --> 01:00:41.261
&gt;&gt; The main idea of Tolstoy, Tolstoy is to
be natural.

01:00:41.261 --> 01:00:41.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:00:41.780 --> 01:00:46.050
&gt;&gt; Everything unnatural as, as Napoleon,
as Helen.

01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:46.618
&gt;&gt; Yes.
yes.

01:00:46.618 --> 01:00:47.540
That's right.

01:00:49.530 --> 01:00:52.629
&gt;&gt; While scorning attempts to better the
world,

01:00:52.629 --> 01:00:56.685
the novel is enthusiastic about the
beauties of daily life.

01:00:56.685 --> 01:00:59.820
[MUSIC]

01:00:59.820 --> 01:01:02.319
The book's message in its affection for

01:01:02.319 --> 01:01:07.790
Natasha is that the elements of beauty
found in ordinary life are important.

01:01:07.790 --> 01:01:12.453
Perhaps in some small way, leaders can
contribute to recognizing and

01:01:12.453 --> 01:01:14.200
increasing that beauty.

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:24.200
[MUSIC]

01:01:25.241 --> 01:01:29.965
This is the Copenhagen Business School, a
site that combines architecture and

01:01:29.965 --> 01:01:35.220
art to create one of the more beautiful
academic environments in the world.

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:40.320
Fin Yungayenson, the long-time rector of
CBS, is an old friend of mine.

01:01:40.320 --> 01:01:44.230
He combines a taste for classical objects
of art with a sense of

01:01:44.230 --> 01:01:50.210
the importance of creating a milieu that
stimulates an aesthetic experience.

01:01:50.210 --> 01:01:54.878
&gt;&gt; One of the conspicuous things about CBS
is the art and architecture.

01:01:54.878 --> 01:01:59.590
You've built some very striking buildings.

01:02:00.740 --> 01:02:01.240
Why?

01:02:03.390 --> 01:02:07.110
&gt;&gt; I think that forms an environment which
gives

01:02:07.110 --> 01:02:12.210
inspiration opportunities because we are
living in it and living as part of it.

01:02:12.210 --> 01:02:14.960
And it makes for more enthusiastic,

01:02:14.960 --> 01:02:19.590
dedicated students who feel themselves
more or less at home here.

01:02:19.590 --> 01:02:23.830
It creates an environment which, makes for
curiosity.

01:02:23.830 --> 01:02:30.100
If you cannot create environments where
you also stimulate inspiration,

01:02:30.100 --> 01:02:36.029
things you cannot explain, emotions which
are of a variety of kinds.

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:42.780
Then you do not develop, entrepreneurs,
people who do things differently.

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:49.710
&gt;&gt; What I hear you say is that a beautiful
setting of architecture and art

01:02:51.180 --> 01:02:58.170
creates a beautiful community and, even if
I could use the term, beautiful people.

01:02:58.170 --> 01:03:03.230
&gt;&gt; It creates, at least, more suitable
conditions for

01:03:03.230 --> 01:03:07.940
the development of beautiful activities
and people.

01:03:07.940 --> 01:03:11.080
Of course, there's no, guarantee,
unfortunately.

01:03:11.080 --> 01:03:16.670
But, but still it stimulates a sense of
doing things at a high level

01:03:16.670 --> 01:03:21.690
because it speaks to your senses and your
non-conscious ways of thinking.

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:25.840
&gt;&gt; I think it is as important to recognize
that there

01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:28.420
are essential elements of beauty.

01:03:28.420 --> 01:03:29.340
&gt;&gt; No, I wouldn't-

01:03:29.340 --> 01:03:35.470
&gt;&gt; As there are to recognize there are
essential answers in Mathematics.

01:03:35.470 --> 01:03:36.060
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:03:36.060 --> 01:03:37.190
&gt;&gt; I'm glad.

01:03:37.190 --> 01:03:38.420
We agree.

01:03:38.420 --> 01:03:40.611
Beauty reigns above all.

01:03:40.611 --> 01:03:41.250
[LAUGH].

01:03:41.250 --> 01:03:47.690
&gt;&gt; It's only the beauty that we, they have
on way that makes any difference.

01:03:47.690 --> 01:03:50.380
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's drink to the beauty.

01:03:50.380 --> 01:03:50.890
&gt;&gt; To beauty.

01:03:50.890 --> 01:03:52.474
[FOREIGN]

01:03:52.474 --> 01:04:02.474
[MUSIC]

01:04:11.555 --> 01:04:13.076
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN].

01:04:13.076 --> 01:04:15.780
&gt;&gt; The earth, the earth it feeds us.

01:04:15.780 --> 01:04:16.430
It gives all.

01:04:16.430 --> 01:04:19.210
You just have to lay your hands to it.

01:04:19.210 --> 01:04:22.090
And there will be fertility, and there
will be everything.

01:04:22.090 --> 01:04:26.820
&gt;&gt; I will never forget the law of in
brotherhood that was between him and me.

01:04:26.820 --> 01:04:29.644
&gt;&gt; That's a very interesting question how
such

01:04:29.644 --> 01:04:32.771
a friendshipped created world was
constructed.

01:04:32.771 --> 01:04:35.283
So crucial to the liquidity of Europe.

01:04:35.283 --> 01:04:37.291
When in fact, people in general don't
trust each other.

01:04:37.291 --> 01:04:38.092
&gt;&gt; [NOISE].

01:04:38.092 --> 01:04:39.694
&gt;&gt; It's about your kiss.

01:04:39.694 --> 01:04:43.315
Your kiss is, as hot as a pancake.

01:04:43.315 --> 01:04:44.904
[MUSIC]

01:04:44.904 --> 01:04:45.446
&gt;&gt; War and

01:04:45.446 --> 01:04:51.590
Peace ensures us that heroic stories of
leaders are the work of our imagination.

01:04:52.830 --> 01:04:56.650
These fables of heroic leaders give
pleasure.

01:04:56.650 --> 01:05:01.650
They can be admired and repeated without
accepting them as reality.

01:05:03.910 --> 01:05:06.140
History is not made by leaders.

01:05:06.140 --> 01:05:13.090
It is made by millions and millions of
peasants and first grade teachers.

01:05:13.090 --> 01:05:15.820
War and Peace argues that the
effectiveness of

01:05:15.820 --> 01:05:19.880
an organization depends less on heroic
leadership than on myriads of

01:05:19.880 --> 01:05:24.721
common people organized into complex
social networks and doing their jobs well.

01:05:24.721 --> 01:05:27.682
[MUSIC]

01:05:27.682 --> 01:05:33.684
It depends on enduring cultures that
nourish and reproduce those capabilities.

01:05:33.684 --> 01:05:43.684
[MUSIC]

01:05:51.053 --> 01:05:56.311
&gt;&gt; Leadership involves allowing the
competence of ordinary people and

01:05:56.311 --> 01:06:01.305
their networks to flower without trying to
strike heroic poses.

01:06:01.305 --> 01:06:05.454
It involves creating objects and
surroundings of beauty and

01:06:05.454 --> 01:06:11.164
encouraging individual contributions to
the elementary grace of everyday life.

01:06:11.164 --> 01:06:12.082
&gt;&gt; That's where the sand is.

01:06:12.082 --> 01:06:13.301
&gt;&gt; That's where the sand is.

01:06:13.301 --> 01:06:14.137
&gt;&gt; Yes.

01:06:14.137 --> 01:06:22.320
&gt;&gt; If you want a darker blue, this is a
darker blue.

01:06:22.320 --> 01:06:25.472
&gt;&gt; I left my doubts go, and I ate pancakes
with you all night.

01:06:25.472 --> 01:06:27.822
[FOREIGN] [LAUGH] So it's not about
pancakes.

01:06:27.822 --> 01:06:30.756
No Russian song is about pancakes.

01:06:30.756 --> 01:06:37.859
In the world of War and Peace, truth and
beauty are precious.

01:06:37.859 --> 01:06:42.550
But beauty is less elusive than truth.

01:06:42.550 --> 01:06:52.550
[MUSIC]

